boardsuite - the board configurations, and what each one is for
===============================================================

	tools/boardsuite/run		check every configuration
	tools/boardsuite/run raspi4	check one
	tools/boardsuite/run -l		list them
	tools/boardsuite/run -b		record baselines instead of checking
	tools/boardsuite/run -v		print the marker set as well

Only the two Pi configurations need the patched build described in
tools/qemu-patches/README; set QEMU= to point at it.  Every patch there
is BCM-specific, and the other seven run on a stock qemu of the right
architecture -- virtarm64 was pointed at the patched binary because it
was to hand, and gives the same 31 markers and 15 known -FAIL on a
packaged qemu-system-aarch64.  Cards are made in $TMPDIR/boardsuite
(override with BOARDSUITE_WORK), blank and sparse, and reused between
runs.

IN A CONTAINER.  docker/boardsuite.sh builds a Debian image with the
packaged qemus, mkfs.vfat, mtools and python3, and runs the seven
configurations that do not want the patched build -- virtarm64,
virtarm64-peer, sifiveu, virt, virt32, virt-peer and peer-mixed.  The
other six are the Pi ones.
The tree is bind-mounted read-only rather than copied -- these five
kernels are committed binaries -- so the image never goes stale when
one is relinked, and mkconfig's absolute ROOT never has to agree with
anything inside it.

That only works while the committed binary is the one the baseline was
recorded against, and it was not: a container run of a clean checkout
gave sifiveu three markers where the baseline says five.  The committed
isifiveu had been linked before the sd device went into its config, and
so had virtarm64's virtarm64.c -- 22 devices where the config names 26.
Neither was noticed because every run until this one used a working
tree where both had since been rebuilt.

It is worth being plain about why those binaries are there at all: they
should not be.  The tree's own .gitignore says so, in its own words --
"kernel binaries: os/inferno/<board>/i<board>" -- and every one of the
eleven tracked kernels matches that rule.  They are tracked because
they were added on 2026-08-23 and the rule arrived on 2026-09-03, and
git does not apply an ignore rule to a file it is already tracking.  So
the quick path rests on an accident, and the stale sifiveu is what an
accident like that looks like when it is leaned on.  The two Pi kernels
were never added, which is the same accident going the other way: it is
why the quick path cannot run those boards.

BUILDING INSTEAD.  docker/boardsuite.sh build builds the tree from
source in the container and runs the suite on what it built: mk, the
libraries, the kenc compilers, limbo and the kernels.  It needs nothing
in the repository that the repository says should not be there.  It
takes minutes rather than seconds, and it needs Linux/arm64 to exist as
a host platform, which it now does.

Two things about the copy it builds in are worth knowing, because both
cost a run.  mkconfig carries an absolute ROOT and a SYSHOST, and both
are the Mac's -- they are rewritten.  And the tree carries the host's
object files: mk is a timestamp build, so it sees lib9/argv0.o newer
than argv0.c, does not rebuild it, and ar puts a Mach-O object into a
Linux archive, after which the link fails with "undefined reference to
argv0" and says nothing about why.  Every .o is deleted from the copy
first.

The seven come back with the same marker sets either way, which is the
result worth having: the same source built by Apple clang and by Debian
gcc, run under two different qemus, says the same thing about the
boards.

It is not the same run twice.  It takes makefat()'s mkfs.vfat and mcopy
path, which the Mac never takes; it runs a qemu Debian built rather
than one built here (7.2 against 10.1.3, which is a difference worth
having); and its host rig, its work directory and its ports are the
container's own, so a container run and a host run can happen at once,
which they cannot today.  The marker sets come back identical to the
recorded ones, which is the point.

Do not record baselines from inside it.  A baseline is what this tree
says the boards do; recording a container's run over it would be the
same "subset written down as the new truth" this file exists to
prevent, arriving from a new direction.

WHY THIS EXISTS.  Two of the mistakes in this tree's own documentation
were made by reading markers off a terminal:

	A harness that stopped QEMU three seconds after the last
	marker reported "38 markers and no -NONE", which read like a
	perfect run.  USB-MOUSE-NONE arrives on the line after the
	last marker, because the mouse check waits out its own
	timeout.  The count was 39 and one of them was a -NONE.

	A marker regex ending in \b missed SD-FS-CREATE-OK, because
	another process printed on the same line and the marker came
	out as "SD-FS-CREATE-OKtrack:".  That looked exactly like a
	regression in the SD path, and was not.

	The same regex began with \b too, and that took longer to
	meet: it needs another process to print immediately BEFORE a
	marker rather than after.  Attaching a mouse to the Pi did it
	-- the usb controller's ctl dump is still printing when
	USB-OK arrives, and the log carries "... hub 2 port 3
	rUSB-OK".  There is no \b between "r" and "U", so the marker
	was not there and the run reported 38 where the board said
	39.  Anyone adding a device to a configuration is changing
	the interleaving, which is how this one was found.

Both are fixed here, once, and the first of them twice.  The obvious fix
-- wait for the board's own marker and then sleep a while -- is still a
race, and this script lost it on its first day out: on a loaded machine
the mouse check reported later than the six seconds allowed, the run
came back one marker short, and it looked exactly like a regression in
the USB path.  So the wait is for the marker that really comes last, on
every board, and the sleep after it is only a flush window.  A board
that never prints it now times out with the name of the line it never
saw, which is a sentence a reader can act on rather than a count that
is quietly wrong.  The marker pattern has no trailing boundary.

WHAT EACH CONFIGURATION COVERS.  The four are not four copies of one
run.  Two of them exist because a branch of sdhcibcm.c is only reached
under a particular card or on a particular controller, and until this
script those branches were covered by boots someone did by hand and
remembered to do:

	raspi3b		The Pi 3B on a 16MB card.  Takes the firmware
			clock branch, because a real Arasan reports no
			base clock and qemu-patches 0026 stopped
			pretending otherwise.  csd v1, because a card
			under 2GB has a v1 CSD.

	raspi3b-4g	The same board on a 4GB card, for the csd v2
			decode.  The capacity field means something
			different in the two versions and the wrong
			decode gives a plausible wrong answer rather
			than an error.

	raspi3b-usb	The same board with a USB stick and the Pi's
			own network adapter.  See THE RICHER RUN.

	raspi4		The Pi 4 on a 16MB card.  Takes the
			capabilities branch, because emmc2 does report
			its base clock and a guest is right to believe
			it there.  Reads the die temperature at the
			model's default, about 25C.

	raspi4-hot	The same board with the sensor set to 85C.
			One reading cannot tell a driver that decodes
			the sensor from one that returns a constant;
			two can.

	raspi4-usb	The richer run on the Pi 4, whose USB is xHCI
			rather than dwc2.  See THE RICHER RUN.

The marker set alone cannot tell these apart -- SDHCI-SPEED-ACCEPTED is
the same marker either way, and THERM-OK is the same marker at any
temperature.  So each configuration also names evidence lines that must
appear in its log, and a run that produces the right markers with the
wrong evidence fails.

THE OTHER BOARDS.  Four more configurations boot the boards this tree
has besides the Pis, each with the qemu its architecture needs -- the
Pi ones need the patched build, the rest are ordinary machines and use
the system qemu:

	virtarm64	QEMU -M virt, cortex-a57, with a virtio-blk-pci
			disk and a virtio-net.  Reaches a shell and runs
			a long suite.  The net device is worth the line:
			without it NET-FAIL, with it NET-OK, and that
			was a known failure until the day something
			attached one.
	sifiveu		QEMU -M sifive_u, no bios.
	virt		QEMU -M virt riscv64, with a virtio-blk disk
			and a virtio-net, both the mmio transport.
	virt32		QEMU -M virt riscv32.
	virt-peer	The same board again, and a second copy of it
			on the other end of a wire.  See TWO GUESTS.
	virtarm64-peer	The same, on the arm64 board: two mmio networks
			and the disk still on PCI, which no other
			configuration exercises together.
	peer-mixed	virt again, with the arm64 board on the other
			end of the wire instead of another riscv64 one.

The transport is not a detail, and getting it wrong looks exactly like a
broken driver.  virtarm64 links sdvirtio10 and no mmio block driver at
all; virt links sdvirtiommio and no PCI one.  Handing virtarm64 a
virtio-blk-device -- the mmio form -- gave it a disk it had no code to
find, and the resulting BLK-FAIL sat in its known-failure list.  The
PCI form alone was not enough either: sdvirtio10 matches on 0x1AF4,
0x1040+type, the modern device id, and a plain virtio-blk-pci
enumerates transitional as 1af4/1001, which that loop walks past
without a word.  It takes disable-legacy=on to make the same disk
visible, and then the driver that was recorded as failing prints
BLK-OK.

The virtio-blk disk is why there is a FAT16 image in the work
directory.  virtinit.b's blkcheck() reads sector 0 of /dev/sdF0/data and
requires the 0x55AA boot signature, and every log of that board in this
tree shows BLK-FAIL -- not because the driver is broken but because
nothing ever gave it a disk.  With one attached it prints BLK-OK.  The
image is made with hdiutil on macOS or mkfs.vfat elsewhere; writing two
signature bytes into a blank file would have satisfied the check and
none of its point, so the script makes a real filesystem or says it
cannot.

The image carries a marker.txt as well, because the disk is asked for
twice.  fsmount() mounts it with dossrv and reads FS-OK out of that
file -- "smoke.sh plants marker.txt", says the comment above it, and
this script did not, so virt's FS-FAIL was the same shape as its
BLK-FAIL one layer up: a check failing because nothing gave it what it
tests.  The file is planted at creation, with hdiutil attach or mcopy;
a host that can make the filesystem but not write into it fails rather
than handing back an image half the check cannot use.

THE HOST END.  virt's network checks do not stop at the wire.
virtinit.b dials tcp!10.0.2.2!54322 and udp!10.0.2.2!54325 -- the slirp
gateway, which is this machine -- writes "hello" and wants "pong" back;
then it announces tcp!*!54323 and udp!*!54326 and waits for someone to
call in.  Nothing here ever answered, so TCP-NET-FAIL "connection
refused" and TCP-IN-FAIL "timeout" were the checks reporting, quite
correctly, that the other end did not exist.  os/inferno/virt/smoke.sh
has answered them since the board was written.  So the suite starts
that rig around the boot: two echo servers, a web server for Charon,
and two clients that keep knocking until the guest has announced, in
one process with one pid to stop, plus the two hostfwd rules the
inbound half needs.  With it the chain runs to the end -- TCP-OK,
TCP-NET-OK, TCP-IN-OK, UDP-OK, UDP-NET-OK, UDP-IN-OK, STYX-OK.

Every socket is bound in that process's main thread before anything is
served, so a port already in use kills it outright and the suite says
which.  The first version bound inside the threads: a shadowed name
took the web server out, the traceback went into a log nobody read,
and the pid stayed alive -- so the guest would have reported the
failure and the reason would have looked like the guest's.  The wait
loop now reads that log too.

The web server on 8765 is the one part of the rig nothing here
exercises.  virtinit.b asks Charon for http://10.0.2.2:8765/smoke.html,
but that is behind the Tk path, which on this board is WM-FAIL, so the
server is started and never asked for anything.  It answers when asked
by hand.  It stays because the check it serves exists and the day the
Tk modules land in that root it will be wanted; it is listed here as
unexercised rather than counted as covered.

Three of these were found in one afternoon: BLK-FAIL wanted a disk,
FS-FAIL wanted a file on it, and the network ones wanted someone to
talk to.  It is worth saying plainly, because all three had been
written down as failures of the board.

TWO GUESTS.  Everything above talks to 10.0.2.2 or 127.0.0.1.  Both are
qemu's user-mode stack, which answers ARP, DHCP, ICMP and TCP out of
one host process -- and would go on answering if the guest's own ARP
were broken, because slirp replies for every address on its network at
once.  So the whole network chain, green as it now is, is one guest
talking to a program written to be agreeable.

virt-peer is the other thing.  Two qemus, joined by -netdev socket
with nothing in between: one listens on a host port, the other
connects to it, and each has a second virtio-net whose frames go
straight to the other guest.  What replies is another copy of this
kernel.  A packet arrives because both ends agreed about ARP, about
addresses and about TCP, not because slirp was obliging.

Both boards that carry virtinit.b have such a configuration, and the
arm64 one is not a copy: its mmio window is 0x200 wide against virt's
0x1000, so bus.1 and bus.2 are 0xA000200 and 0xA000400 there rather
than 0x10002000 and 0x10003000, and its disk stays on PCI while both
its networks are mmio -- a combination nothing else here boots.

The two guests run the same code and tell themselves apart by the MAC
the harness gives the peer link: 52:54:00:aa:aa:01 announces and answers
on 192.168.77.1, :02 dials and asks from .2, and the address comes off
the same byte so nothing has to be passed in.  Both then read their own
/net/arp and check the other's address is in it, which is the part
slirp could never have taught them.  A boot with no such device --
every other one here -- prints PEER-NONE and moves on.

peer-mixed is the one worth arguing for.  Both ends of the other two
pairs are the same kernel, so a bug in how this tree writes an ARP
packet or lays out an IP header is a bug both ends share, and they
would agree with each other perfectly while agreeing with nothing else.
Its two ends are a riscv64 kernel and an aarch64 one, built by
different compilers for different word orders and run by different
emulators, and they still complete the exchange and each finish with
the other in their own /net/arp.

It is otherwise virt-peer exactly -- same board, same host rig, same
hostfwd -- so its marker set has to come out identical, and it does:
44 markers and the same 12 known -FAIL.  The only thing changed is who
is on the other end.  config_peer_kernel and config_peer_qemu exist for
it, since the second guest there is a different binary run by a
different emulator.

Each pair has its own socket port -- 14444, 14445 and 14446 -- because
they run one after the other in a full pass and a port still in
TIME_WAIT from the last one is a race this suite has already paid for
once.

The second guest is rig, not a board: no baseline, no marker set, no
disk, and its own slirp checks fail because the hostfwd rules belong to
the guest being checked.  What it has to say is three lines, and
config_peer_evidence names them; a run where only one side of the
conversation happened fails.

Starting it is not a matter of putting it second.  qemu's socket netdev
is not patient: a connect= that is refused makes qemu exit at once, so
launching the second guest in the same breath as the first is a race
against the first one's bind, and it lost about one run in three.  The
port cannot be probed to find out, either -- the listening end accepts
exactly one peer, and the probe would be it.  So the second guest is
given a moment, started, and started again if it did not survive its
first second; a configuration whose other guest will not stay up says
so rather than reporting the silence as the board's.

Two details that took a boot each to find.  The peer check runs before
ipcheck() rather than after: the whole slirp chain runs inside
ipcheck(), so a peercheck() behind it announces a hundred lines later
on the guest that has the host rig than on the guest that does not, and
the dialler gives up before the listener is up.  And every virtio
device in that configuration names its bus, because with two networks
ether0 has to be the slirp one and ether1 the peer, and qemu hands out
unnamed mmio slots from the top down -- leaving it to command-line
order works until it does not.

Finding the second interface at all needs one more thing: devether's
attach spec names the controller, so "#l" with no spec is ether0 and
shows nothing else.  /net/ether1 does not exist however many the kernel
printed at boot until "#l1" is bound after it.

KNOWN FAILURES.  A configuration may have a <config>.expected file
listing the markers known to fail, and the run checks that set as
strictly as it checks the marker set.  A new -FAIL fails the run.  So
does one that went away, because a debt that got paid should be written
down rather than silently forgotten.  What is in them, and why:

	Both boards	TK, WM, WMSRV, WM-STRESS, FT, TB, SSL, AUTH,
			HEAVY, CMD-STRESS -- each wants a module the
			root file list does not carry, and says "file
			does not exist" or "unknown device".  Adding
			them is a change to those kernels' roots, not
			to this script.

	virt		DNS-FAIL: missing /dis/ndb/dns.dis, the same
			kind.  It is newly visible rather than new --
			nothing reached dnscheck() until IP-OK started
			passing.

	virtarm64	DHCP-FAIL, and with it everything downstream:
			the dhcpclient module is not in that root
			either, so the whole ip chain after NET-OK is
			unreached rather than broken.  TMP-FAIL and
			FS-MKDIR-FAIL are one failure twice: memfs is
			missing, so /tmp is not writable, so the
			directory fsmount() makes there cannot be made.

Nothing in that list is a guess.  Each was read off the board's own
error line, and the two that turned out not to belong -- BLK-FAIL and
FS-FAIL -- were taken out by fixing them.

A passing run says how many known failures it carried -- "ok 31 markers
and 15 known -FAIL" -- because a suite that reports a board with
fifteen broken checks as simply "ok" is lying by omission.  These lists
are debt, not a baseline to be comfortable with.

THE RICHER RUN.  raspi3b and raspi3b-4g have no USB disk and no USB
ethernet, so DISK-NONE and ETHER-NONE are the right answers there and
are in their baselines.  raspi3b-usb is the same board with both
attached: a FAT16 stick behind usb-storage, and usb-lan9514 -- this
tree's own model, qemu-patches 0003 -- with a user network behind it.
It gives 39 markers where the plain board gives 35, the six the plan
document predicted in place of the two -NONEs.

usb-lan9514 rather than qemu's usb-net is the point of it: usb-net is
CDC ECM, and the SMSC half of appl/cmd/usb/etherusb.b, about a thousand
lines of vendor requests and command words and receive status, has no
other way to execute.  ETHER-DHCP-OK there is a DHCP exchange that
crossed the USB link in both directions.

Both of them also carry a mouse, and it is the reason USB-MOUSE-NONE
finally leaves a baseline in this tree.  That check has never once run
to completion: with nothing attached it says -NONE, which is the right
answer and the only one it has ever given.  A usb-mouse alone does not
help either -- an emulated mouse nobody moves says nothing -- so the
suite opens qemu's monitor on a TCP port and sends, once a second, a
motion in x and a press and release of the right and middle buttons,
which is exactly what mousecheck() waits for.  It repeats because the
window opens when the guest opens /dev/pointer and nothing outside the
guest knows when that is.

USB-MOUSE-OK ... buttons 6 is the evidence line: 6 is right (4) and
middle (2) together, and the number is what says which direction
usbd's HID-to-devpointer remap went.  Both boards report 6.

The keyboard is the other half of the same path and had even less to
show for itself: its code printed one line per character for as long as
the board ran, which is a report and not a check -- a reader had to
decide for themselves whether what arrived was right, and no log said
either way.  It is kbdcheck() now, it waits for the word the comment
above its own call has always named, and it says USB-KBD-OK, -NONE or
-FAIL.  The harness types "hello" once a second alongside the mouse
events.  Both boards answer "USB-KBD-OK 5 chars, hello".

The two checks time out at twenty seconds and twenty-five, and the
difference is deliberate: both are spawned together and both report
-NONE by timing out, so on a board with neither device attached the
markers would race and config_last would name one that is not last.
The keyboard goes first.  Checked under load -- a container suite
running beside it -- and the order held.

Attaching it moved config_last.  With a mouse the check reports as soon
as it has what it wants instead of waiting out its 25-second timeout,
so USB-MOUSE -- the marker that is last on every other Pi
configuration, precisely because of that timeout -- is not last on
these two.  The shell's marker is.

raspi4-usb is the same again on the Pi 4, and worth its own run for one
reason: that board's USB is xHCI where the Pi 3's is dwc2, so the same
six markers, and the mouse, are reached over a different host
controller.  It gives 36
where the plain Pi 4 gives 32 -- four fewer than the Pi 3 pair because
the Pi 4 baseline is four markers shorter to begin with, not because
anything is missing here.

Its stick is made fresh every run, unlike every other image here,
because the check that reads it also writes to it: diskcheck() creates
pi.txt, so a second run would find two files and DISK-READ-OK would
report whichever the directory read returned first.  That discipline is
checked rather than trusted -- one of the evidence lines requires the
listing to name usb.txt and nothing else, and a run with the remake
taken out fails on it with the same 39 markers, which is what a rule
worth writing down looks like.

The tail of that line is [^ ]*$ rather than a bare $, because the
board's console ends its lines with CR LF: an anchor placed straight
after the name matches nothing at all.  It cost a run to find, and it
applies to every evidence line anyone adds here.

And nothing on a disk in this suite may be shaped like a marker.  The
first version of this configuration reported FS-OK -- a marker no check
on that board prints -- because the stick carried the virt image's
marker.txt and DISK-READ-OK prints the contents of the file it read.
The extractor reads the log, not the board's intentions.

Nothing on real hardware is covered by any of this: every marker below
is qemu-tier evidence, and the two ACCEPTED ones stay ACCEPTED because
nothing in the model times a transfer against SDCLK.

BASELINES.  One file per configuration, holding the marker set that
run produces.  They are checked in so that a change to them is a change
someone had to make and explain, which is the point.

Record with -b.  It refuses to write a baseline smaller than the one
already there, and prints which markers went missing -- a run that
stopped early produces a subset, and recording a subset is a regression
written down as the new truth, which is the failure this script exists
to prevent.  -b -f records it anyway, for when the markers really did
go away and you mean it.

That guard compares counts, not sets, so a run that swapped one marker
for another of the same name-count walks through it.  That is not
hypothetical either: virtarm64's BLK-FAIL became BLK-OK at no change in
count.  What caught it was the check the guard does not do -- the diff
against the recorded set, which every ordinary run makes -- so the hole
is covered in practice by the thing that runs far more often.  Worth
knowing rather than worth more code.

RETRIES.  Several of these checks time themselves against the system
timer and compare a count to what the elapsed time predicts.  Host load
only ever costs them events, never gains them, so a machine that is
busy elsewhere makes ARMTIMER, ARMTIMER-INTR and SYSTMR report -FAIL
for a board that is fine.  That is not hypothetical: running this suite
alongside a qemu test suite produced exactly those three, and the same
configuration passed on its own a minute later.  It is the one-sided
error the checks themselves handle by taking a best of several, arriving
one level up.

So a configuration that fails is booted once more, and a run that passes
the second time says so -- "on the second boot; the first was starved".
A real regression fails twice.  A retry that is not reported is a suite
that hides a flake, which is worse than a suite that fails.

A run fails on three things: a marker set that differs from the
baseline, a -FAIL marker in it, or a missing evidence line.  It is the
marker form that is checked, not prose: a check that printed the word
failure without printing a -FAIL marker would not be caught here, and
that is the same definition the rest of the tree uses.
